@startuml
title One token stream, two candidate readings of `6 * 3 / 2` — a SHARED level decides

skinparam backgroundColor #FEFEFE
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowColor #374151
skinparam ArrowThickness 1.3
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #1F2937
  FontColor #111827
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #F9FAFB
  BorderColor #9CA3AF
}

rectangle "**TOKEN STREAM** (identical for both readings)\n`6`   `*`   `3`   `/`   `2`" as TOK #F3F4F6

rectangle "READING A — **ELECTED** (left-to-right)" as A #DCFCE7 {
  rectangle "**DivInt** · `/` · <latex>(\ell,r) = (8,9)</latex>" as A_DIV #DBEAFE
  rectangle "**MulInt** · `*` · <latex>(\ell,r) = (8,9)</latex>" as A_MUL #DBEAFE
  rectangle "**NumLit 6**" as A_6 #E0E7FF
  rectangle "**NumLit 3**" as A_3 #E0E7FF
  rectangle "**NumLit 2**" as A_2 #E0E7FF
  rectangle "value <latex>= \lfloor (6 \times 3)/2 \rfloor = \lfloor 18/2 \rfloor = 9</latex>\nre-printed surface: `6 * 3 / 2` (**unchanged**)" as A_VAL #FEF3C7
}

rectangle "READING B — **NEVER BUILT**" as B #FEE2E2 {
  rectangle "--**MulInt** `*` outermost--" as B_MUL #FCA5A5
  rectangle "--**DivInt** `/` inner--" as B_DIV #FCA5A5
  rectangle "--**NumLit 6**--" as B_6 #FECACA
  rectangle "--**NumLit 3**--" as B_3 #FECACA
  rectangle "--**NumLit 2**--" as B_2 #FECACA
  rectangle "would be <latex>6 \times \lfloor 3/2 \rfloor = 6 \times 1 = 6</latex>\nre-printed surface: `6 * (3 / 2)` (**bracketed**)" as B_VAL #FEF3C7
}

TOK -down-> A_MUL : precedence climbing\nabsorbs `*` at floor 0
TOK -down-> B_MUL : the right-nested reading

A_MUL -down-> A_6 : child 0
A_MUL -down-> A_3 : child 1, parsed at floor <latex>r = 9</latex>;\n`/` has <latex>\ell = 8 < 9</latex>, so it is NOT absorbed here
A_DIV -down-> A_MUL : `/` re-enters the OUTER loop and takes\nthe completed `6 * 3` as its left operand
A_DIV -down-> A_2
A_DIV -down-> A_VAL

B_MUL -down-> B_6
B_MUL -down-> B_DIV
B_DIV -down-> B_3
B_DIV -down-> B_2
B_MUL -down-> B_VAL

note right of B_MUL
  To build this, `/` would have to be absorbed
  into the RIGHT OPERAND of `*`. It cannot: the
  gate is <latex>\ell(\mathtt{/}) \ge \mathrm{floor}</latex>, i.e. <latex>8 \ge 9</latex>, which
  is FALSE.

  This is the general mechanism of LEFT
  associativity. Every member of a level offers
  <latex>\ell = p</latex> and demands <latex>r = p+1</latex> of its right
  operand, and <latex>p < p+1</latex>, so no member can ever
  nest inside another member's right operand.
end note

note right of A_VAL
  The printer agrees, and that agreement is
  the check. `display.rs` writes reading A as
  `6 * 3 / 2` (no bracket: the LEFT child's
  <latex>\ell = 8</latex> meets its inherited floor of 8)
  and reading B as `6 * (3 / 2)`
  (bracket forced: the RIGHT child's <latex>\ell = 8</latex>
  fails its inherited floor of 9).
  Parser and printer partition the two
  readings by the SAME numbers.
end note

legend bottom
  `*` opens the multiplicative level; `/` and `%` carry the `same` annotation,
  so all three share it. `analyze_binding_powers` advances its counter once per
  LEVEL, handing out <latex>2, 4, 6, \ldots</latex> — an UNANNOTATED rule binds tighter than
  the one before it, and a `same`-annotated rule ties with it.

  HISTORICAL: until 2026-07-28 the counter advanced once per RULE in BOTH
  associativity arms, so equal precedence was unrepresentable — rules
  <latex>i < j</latex> could share an <latex>\ell</latex> only if <latex>2(j-i) = 1</latex>. `*` was <latex>(20,21)</latex> and `/`
  was <latex>(22,23)</latex>, reading B was the ELECTED one, and `6 * 3 / 2` evaluated to 6.
endlegend

@enduml
